  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We approach the problem through multi-scale optimization and transformation invariants to escape local optima. The previous code's local minima are addressed by introducing 12-dimensional transformation groups (frequency-space representations) and adaptive-shrinkage coordinate search. This uses exploding gradients and tempered simulated annealing to enforce global exploration, while maintaining focus on critical lags identified by local sensitivity analysis. We distinguish between two strategies: (i) direct gradient projection with dual-variable scaling (proven but incomplete), and (ii) hybrid dual/multi-scale methods that frame the correlation peak localization as a constrained quadratic minimization. The core contribution is a fitness landscape transformation that separates lag-coefficient interactions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Variables: h \u2208 R^n with 0\u2264hi\u22641, \u2211h_i = T = 2.0/n\n   Objective: C_5(h) = (1/\u0394t) * max_{k\u2208[-1.5,1.5]} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx, where \u0394t=2.0/n\n   Constraints: (a) Integral constraint (b) box constraints\n\n   We reformulate via characteristic function smoothing (proposed Cut-and-Project Krawtchouk-like transform) and use:\n   - Chordal distance minimization on the correlation manifold\n   - Lag coefficients separation: C5 = \u2211_{k\u2208\u039b} c_k |\u03c9_{k}|^2 + orthogonalization gaps penalty\n   The search space is 12-dimensional with a tempered exploration schedule applied to |\u03c9_{k}| matrices.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   - Initialize h0 from initial_h_values (if available)\n   - Precompute lag-space projections \u039b = [-\u03b2,\u03b2]\u00d7[0,2] where \u03b2=0.5 (doubled from parent)\n   - Define fitness: F(h) = max_k corr(k) with a smoothing kernel \u03c3=0.05\n   - Perform: \n        Phase1: Multi-scale gradient outbreak (L-BFGS-B with \u03b7=0.1)\n        Phase2: Cycle-coordinate shrinking (with l=5 direction vectors)\n        Phase3: Transform dual variables through Krawtchouk features (K_quad(n=10))\n        \u21d2 Multiphase loop until time limit\n\n   Helper functions:\n   - `smooth_corr(h, \u03c3=0.05)`: Gauss-smoothed correlation\n   - `project_h_constrained(h)`: Maintains integral & boxes via singular value projection\n   - `LagVectorizer(h)`: Maps h \u21a6 (FFT{h}, LagFilter convolutions)\n\n   The search continues until:\n   - Evaluation budget exceeded\n   - Convergence based on lag-space energy scaling\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Initialization: Use the provided initial_h_values if available\n   - Lag selection: Utilize `max(np.correlate(...))` with adaptive \u03c3 based on gradient\n   - Ten variations: All combinations of:\n        \u2022 Phase-length ratio: \u03b1\u2208{0.25,0.5,1.0}\n        \u2022 Projection robustness tuning: \u211d\u2192\u211d\u207a scaling\n        \u2022 Noise regimen: Gamma jump vs Cauchy-normal\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Timings: Baseline runs projected to 800s (saving 300 to heuristics)\n   - Monitoring: Time checks every 50 iterations\n   - Temperature drop: T(t) = T0 * exp(-t/\u03c4) with \u03c4=700s\n   - Recovery: fallback to provided parent if NaN in energy\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Final h satisfies 0\u2264hi\u22641 and sum(h)=n/2\n   - All intermediate projected candidates are feasible\n   - Energy computed as: E = float(np.max(np.correlate(h,1.0-h,mode='full')))*dx\n   - Returned solution must show C_5 < current record (executed separately)\n\n   Landing vectors are constructed via:\n      h_fallback = initial_h_values (if parent exists)\n      h_final = coordinate_descent(best_candidate) + L_BFGS_refinement\n\n   Final score checked against previous record.",
